<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashot I of Iberia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 826</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagratuni-dynasty">Bagratuni dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashot I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> was the Bagratid prince who established his family as the leading power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. Driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, he moved to Tao-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klarjeti">Klarjeti</a> in the south-west early in the 9th century, rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artanuji">Artanuji</a> and received the Byzantine title of kouropalates. His descendants, among them Adarnase IV, restored Georgian kingship and later united the country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashot-i-of-iberia.jpg" alt="Ashot I of Iberia" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mostafameraji · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Georgian_National_Museum_HDR_Photos_(LG_G22)_%D9%85%D9%88%D8%B2%D9%87_%D9%85%D9%84%DB%8C_%DA%AF%D8%B1%D8%AC%D8%B3%D8%AA%D8%A7%D9%86%D8%8C_%D8%AA%D9%81%D9%84%DB%8C%D8%B3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ashot, called the Great, was the son of the Iberian nobleman Adarnase, who had founded the hereditary Bagratid fief in Tao-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klarjeti">Klarjeti</a>, in what is now north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, and left his son wide lands acquired when the Guaramid and Chosroid lines died out. Ashot first tried to win a foothold in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, Shida Kartli, but Arab control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> defeated him. He was the first of the Bagratid family to become presiding prince of Iberia, around 813.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In his patrimonial duchy of Klarjeti Ashot restored the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artanuji">Artanuji</a>, said to have been built by King Vakhtang I Gorgasali in the 5th century, and placed himself under Byzantine protection. To revive a country devastated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and by cholera, he supported the monastic communities founded by Grigol Khandzteli and encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> to settle in the region, so that the political and religious centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> moved to the south-west.</p><p>From Tao-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klarjeti">Klarjeti</a> he took much of the land between Tao and Shida Kartli, including Kola, Artani, Javakheti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samtskhe">Samtskhe</a> and Trialeti; only Kakheti escaped him. Around 818 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> recognised him as prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> to counter the rebellious emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a>, Isma'il ibn Shu'aib, who had the support of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigol-of-kakheti">Grigol of Kakheti</a> and the highland Mtiulians and Tsanars. Joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> of Abkhazia, Ashot defeated the emir on the Ksani and pushed the Kakhetians out of central Iberia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The family's fortunes turned when Khalid ibn Yazid, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s viceroy of Arminiya, reasserted Arab authority through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>. Driven from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, Ashot fell back to the Nigali valley, where renegades killed him at the altar of a church. His lands were divided among his three sons, Bagrat I, Adarnase II and Guaram, and a daughter married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> of Abkhazia. Remembered as a patron of Christian culture and a friend of the church, Ashot has been canonised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgian-orthodox-church">Georgian Orthodox Church</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ashot I of Iberia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bagratid prince <b>Ashot I</b> moved the family's base to Tao-Klarjeti in the early 9th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-iberia">Principality of Iberia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Nigali valley, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.2,41.84&amp;year=816" aria-label="Nigali valley on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5572 694 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ashot I of Iberia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6022 964h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6022 964h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.2,41.84&amp;year=816">What was here in 816 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
